WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:04.880
Film Courage: You’ve said that acting allows
you to be many things that in your personal

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:12.120
life you may not be able to be but on camera
you can be that.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.320
I’m paragraphing this.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:23.289
Bill Duke, actor, filmmaker, author: I think
all of us are everything and people say how

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:32.359
can you say that I’m a woman with a child,
I’m not a bad person.

00:00:32.359 --> 00:00:35.581
Okay…suppose someone just came up and hit
your baby in the head with hammer.

00:00:35.581 --> 00:00:44.800
What would you call your response then if
you picked that hammer up.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:57.769
I’m not saying you’re anything but you’re
everything and if you’re hired to do a job

00:00:57.769 --> 00:01:09.000
the director doesn’t hire you act like that
person, the director hires you to become that

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.460
person.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.040
And that something that most people don’t
understand about acting, acting is not…that

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:23.830
word is annoying because acting is not pretending,
acting is becoming.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:30.010
It’s surrendering to the spirit, whatever
that character you are describing is.

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:37.351
There is a thing called stage fright, that’s
where you are right in the middle, where you

00:01:37.351 --> 00:01:47.100
are giving part of yourself up to be part
of the character, but your ego and fear and

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:55.140
paranoia is watching how you give it up and
tries to control the shape of that giving

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.480
up.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:08.670
Real actors like the ones I adore, Kate Winslet,
Meryl Streeps, Denzel when he is really in

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:24.110
it and, the Sam Jacksons, Philip Seymour Hoffmans
and the Jeffrey Rights and when those people

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:32.960
go there is nobody there except that character,
that’s admirable and that’s courage.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:38.260
People don’t understand because some days
you’re not in control of it, you have to

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:39.260
ride.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.580
You have to trust.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:46.020
And the person you trust is the director “Am
I riding right?”

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:55.030
You don’t stand outside because you don’t
stand outside and look at your ride.

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:57.280
And that’s what real great actors do.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.840
Philip Seymour Hoffman is one of my favorite
actors.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:09.060
Film Courage: Can someone still be great and
have stage fright?

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:18.250
Bill: The can be great and have stage fright?

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:25.460
They can be really good and have stage fright.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:34.420
To surrender totally to the moment and to
totally respond to what the other person you

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:40.030
are acting with is doing, that takes courage.

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:47.160
Your not watching yourself anymore, you’re
watching the person you are with in the scene.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:52.120
You’re not watching how you react to what
they do.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.819
You’re responding like we are talking right
now.

00:03:54.819 --> 00:03:58.819
I’m not trying to be a special anything.

00:03:58.819 --> 00:04:02.720
I’m just responding to what you are asking
me.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.800
That’s what acting should be.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:12.990
Film Courage: So overcoming stage fright being
less sort of this defiant rebel “Oh, I don’t

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:13.990
care what people think of me.”

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:23.699
And more being so immersed in the moment that
all of that is…you don’t see it?

00:04:23.699 --> 00:04:33.500
Bill: One of my great acting teachers when
I was a very young actor asked a simple question

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:40.460
and he said “I’m going to make it real
simple for you.”

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:48.800
He said It’s like falling into darkness
backward.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:54.669
Imagine just falling into darkness backward.

00:04:54.669 --> 00:05:04.789
No control of your landing, if you’re going
to be caught or not, just falling.

00:05:04.789 --> 00:05:11.589
And then he says “Well suppose there’s
a rock back there?

00:05:11.589 --> 00:05:22.919
Or suppose there is an I don’t know no pillows
and just hard floor?

00:05:22.919 --> 00:05:33.759
The writer is saying to you “I want you
to fall into darkness backward.

00:05:33.759 --> 00:05:40.550
I’m giving you all the description about
who this character is.

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:45.830
We’ve had rehearsal and discussions on who
this human being is.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:51.199
I do not want you to describe them to me.

00:05:51.199 --> 00:06:01.180
I want you to become them by falling into
darkness backward.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:01.720
Can you do that?”

